{% import "_macros.j2" as m %}
{# 2–4 terms explained side by side. Each column is **a title, a description, and then free
   text** — and the free text really is free: a list renders as bullets, a string as paragraphs,
   because the source shapes differ per column (one term is explained in three bullets, its
   neighbour in two paragraphs). Forcing a list on both would invent a structure the content does
   not have — which is the same mistake as reading these columns as a table.
   The columns deliberately share no row structure; that is what separates this from
   `value-columns`, where the cells align row by row over a shared factor.
   Each column is a **panel with a content-matched icon**, like every other labeled set in the
   deck — three columns of naked prose read as one wall of text, with nothing saying the three
   are parallel. `emphasis` marks the column the slide is *about* (not the winner); CSS swaps its
   panel for the accent one and inverts it. How many columns there are, and how they tighten as that count grows, is
   count-driven CSS — nothing here branches on it. #}
{% call m.stage(s) %}
{% if s.subtitle %}<p class="ccsub">{{ s.subtitle }}</p>{% endif %}
<div class="conccols">
{% for c in s.columns %}
<div class="conccol{{ ' em' if c.emphasis else '' }}{{ m.frag(s) }}">
{% if c.icon %}{{ icon(c.icon) }}{% endif %}
<h3>{{ c.label | nocolon }}</h3>
{% if c.body %}<p class="ccdef">{{ c.body }}</p>{% endif %}
{% if c.text_label %}<p class="ccflab">{{ c.text_label | nocolon }}</p>{% endif %}
{% if c.text is string %}
{% for para in c.text.split("\n") if para.strip() %}<p class="ccfree">{{ para }}</p>{% endfor %}
{% elif c.text %}
<ul class="ccfeat">{% for f in c.text %}<li>{{ f }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
{% endif %}
{% if c.example %}<p class="ccex">{{ c.example }}</p>{% endif %}
</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% endcall %}
